What is an Ansible Playbook?
An Ansible playbook is a YAML file that defines a set of tasks to be executed on remote systems.
It’s a way to automate tasks like software installation, configuration changes, and system management. Think of it as a recipe for your infrastructure! 📜🍽️

Key Components of a Playbook
Play: A play defines a set of tasks to be executed on a group of hosts. It’s like a chapter in your book of automation. 📖
Tasks: Tasks are the individual actions within a play. Each task uses an Ansible module to perform an action. 🛠️
Modules: Modules are the building blocks of tasks. They are predefined functions that Ansible uses to perform actions like installing packages, copying files, and more. 🔑
Variables: Variables store data that can be reused in playbooks. They make your playbooks flexible and dynamic.
Handlers: Handlers are special tasks that only run when notified by other tasks. They are great for managing services and performing actions only when changes occur.

YAML stands for YAML Ain't Markup Language.
It is a human-readable format used to write configuration and data.
In Ansible, playbooks are written in YAML.


- name: Install and start nginx              This is the name of the Play.    It describes the overall work that this play will do.
  hosts: webservers                          we can give  all,single machine means give ip address,or a particular group
  remote_user: ubuntu                        which Linux user Ansible uses to log in to the remote machine.    Remote EC2 → AWS EC2 machine where Ansible performs the tasks.manged node
  become: yes                                This tells Ansible to perform tasks with sudo/root privileges.certain task required some privilage access

  vars:                                        Later we can use it as:  "{{ package_name }}"
    package_name: nginx                       So instead of writing nginx everywhere, we use the variable.

  tasks:                                      This is the main section where we tell Ansible what actions to perform.

    - name: Install nginx                             This is the name of the Task.   It describes one particular action inside the play.
      apt:                                            apt is the Ansible module.
        name: "{{ package_name }}"                     Install nginx
        state: present                           to install
     
    - name: Copy nginx configuration
      copy:
        src: nginx.conf                       file coming from control machine
        dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf               where file is placed on EC2
        mode: '0644'                       premission   In 0755, the first 0 is not a permission.    It tells Linux that 755 is written in octal (base-8) notation.
      notify: Restart nginx                  "If the above task actually changes something, call the handler named Restart nginx." It does not mean restart nginx every time.

    - name: Start nginx                 This task uses the service module.
      service:
        name: "{{ package_name }}"
        state: started                 to start the nginx.  Make sure nginx is running.
        enabled: yes            Start nginx automatically when the server boots.

  handlers:                         "When I am notified, restart nginx."   Handlers are special tasks.  They normally don't run just because they are written in the playbook.
                                They run when another task notifies them.
    - name: Restart nginx
      service:
        name: nginx                    
        state: restarted                Restart the nginx service.  But it happens only when the task that notified it reports a change.

