What happens?
Ansible connects to every server in your inventory:
Control Node
     |
     +---- Server 1 → nginx + git + vim + wget
     |
     +---- Server 2 → nginx + git + vim + wget
     |
     +---- Server 3 → nginx + git + vim + wget


If you mean ansible.builtin.package, it is not yum.
It is a generic Ansible module for installing packages.

Think of it this way
On different Linux systems, different package managers are used:

RHEL / CentOS  → yum / dnf
Ubuntu         → apt
Ansible gives you a common module:  ansible.builtin.package:


ansible.builtin.package: can work with both Ubuntu and RHEL for normal package installation.  Ansible then uses the appropriate package manager on the target machine.
Example:
- name: Install nginx
  hosts: all
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Install nginx
      ansible.builtin.package:
        name: nginx
        state: present

If your inventory has:
Ubuntu server
RHEL server

Ansible will use the appropriate package manager on each:

Ubuntu
  ↓
package module
  ↓
apt

RHEL
  ↓
package module
  ↓
dnf/yum

So you don't need to write separate apt and yum tasks for this basic case.

One thing to remember: package is generic, but package names can differ between operating systems. For example,
some software may be called one thing on Ubuntu and another on RHEL. In that situation, you may need OS-specific tasks.

