tagging

In Ansible, tags are used to run only specific tasks from a playbook instead of running every task.

Example
- name: Configure server
  hosts: all
  become: yes

  tasks:

    - name: Install nginx
      package:
        name: nginx
        state: present
      tags:
        - install

    - name: Start nginx
      service:
        name: nginx
        state: started
      tags:
        - service

    - name: Copy configuration
      copy:
        src: nginx.conf
        dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf
      tags:
        - config

Now you have 3 tags:

install
service
config

Run only the install task     ansible-playbook playbook.yml --tags install
Only this runs:  Install nginx

Run only service        ansible-playbook playbook.yml --tags service
Only:  Start nginx


Run multiple tags
ansible-playbook playbook.yml --tags "install,service"
Both tasks run.

Why use tags?
Imagine your playbook has 20 tasks, but you only want to install packages:
20 tasks
   ↓
--tags install
   ↓
Only installation tasks



Suppose your playbook has 20 tasks and you add a 21st task.
If you run:  ansible-playbook playbook.yml
Ansible will check all 21 tasks. It does not automatically skip the first 20 just because they ran before.
But the important part is:  Ansible checks the current state before changing anything.


If you only want to run the new task
This is where tags are useful.

Give task 21 a tag:
- name: Install vim
  package:
    name: vim
    state: present
  tags:
    - new_task

Then:  ansible-playbook playbook.yml --tags new_task
Now only the tagged task runs.





