If you want only task 21, use a tag:


If you add a 21st task and run the whole playbook, Ansible will go through all 21 tasks.
It will not automatically run only the new task.
But for the old 20 tasks, Ansible checks whether their desired state is already achieved.

Example:
20 tasks already completed
        +
1 new task
        ↓
Run playbook
        ↓
Task 1  → check → already done → no change
Task 2  → check → already done → no change
...
Task 20 → check → already done → no change
Task 21 → check → not done → execute

So the answer is:  Ansible checks all 21 tasks, but normally only performs a change for the new task (and any old task whose state has changed).
If you want to skip checking the old 20 completely, use a tag on task 21:  ansible-playbook playbook.yml --tags new_task
Then only task 21 is selected and executed.

